 "callout": "<b>Why this sits alongside the GA4 dashboard.</b> This week the two measures point in opposite directions, and covers are the fuller read. GA4 bookings are <b>down 4.7% YoY</b> while covers are <b>up 23.8% YoY</b>, or <b>up 11.1%</b> on a like-for-like basis once Shiro Tai Kwun, which has no 2025 records, is taken out. GA4 only fires on our own website, which is <b>23 percent</b> of Hong Kong covers. It never sees <b>OpenRice</b> (<b>25 percent</b>), and it never sees walk-ins and the reception and reservations desks, which together are <b>44 percent</b>. Covers came in at <b>9,873</b>, <b>up 2.4 percent</b> week on week, so demand grew in covers terms even though GA4 bookings fell 6.8 percent. <b>OpenRice</b> had its strongest week of the eight at <b>2,482</b> covers, up 20.3 percent week on week, while <b>Google Reserve</b> fell to <b>784</b> from 958. That pair of moves is a booking handoff inside the third-party channel, not a change in demand. <b>Hutong HK</b> reception rose to <b>432</b> from 129 on two large forward-dated event bookings, 110 covers for 20 November and 89 for 14 February 2027. That is real demand booked early, not a data load. <b>Cantina</b> reception fell back to <b>171</b> from 346 after last week&rsquo;s December event run. <b>Shiro Pacific Place</b> is like for like at <b>1,918</b> covers against 1,583 last year, while <b>Shiro Tai Kwun</b> (1,012 covers) has no 2025 records, so its year-on-year reads n/a. <b>Vesu</b> (614 covers) and <b>The Canton Library</b> (411) sit below the portfolio line and are not in these totals.",
